# Splitting Comma-Delimited Strings in Laravel Views

You are currently displaying a database column containing raw text like `{ 1234, normal, r4r3r2 }` as a single list item. To transform this into a table with three distinct columns, you need to parse the string within your Blade view and map the resulting segments to individual cells. This is a common requirement when dealing with legacy data or quick prototypes where database normalization hasn't happened yet.

The core logic involves two steps: splitting the string by the delimiter (in this case, a comma) and then filtering out any whitespace to clean up the output. You can achieve this using PHP's native `explode()` function directly inside your Blade loop.

Update your `home.blade.php` file to handle the parsing inline. Since your data includes curly braces `{ }`, you must strip those characters before splitting, or they will appear as part of the first and last values. Here is a robust approach:

```blade
<ol>
   @foreach($test as $row)
     <li>{{ str_replace(['{', '}'], '', $row->data) | explode(', ') }}</li>
   @endforeach
</ol>
```

However, this still renders the result inside a single `<li>` tag. To get a table structure, you need to iterate over the exploded array and render each item in its own `<td>`. You can achieve this by adding another loop or using `array_column` logic. A cleaner pattern for displaying three specific columns is to map the split values directly:

```blade
<table>
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Value 1</th>
            <th>Value 2</th>
            <th>Value 3</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        @foreach($test as $row)
            @php
                $cleanData = str_replace(['{', '}'], '', $row->data);
                $parts = array_filter(array_map('trim', explode(',', $cleanData)));
            @endphp

            <tr>
                <td>{{ $parts[0] ?? '' }}</td>
                <td>{{ $parts[1] ?? '' }}</td>
                <td>{{ $parts[2] ?? '' }}</td>
            </tr>
        @endforeach
    </tbody>
</table>
```

This inline PHP block safely handles cases where the split might result in fewer than three values, preventing "undefined array key" errors by using the `??` operator.

### Handling Data Consistency and Edge Cases

While splitting strings is straightforward, relying on this pattern for production systems introduces technical debt. The string format `{ 1234, normal, r4r3r2 }` implies a lack of normalization in your database schema. If you anticipate the column growing beyond three items or changing formats frequently, hardcoding indices like `$parts[2]` becomes fragile.

A more dynamic approach is to render all parts regardless of count:

```blade
<tr>
    @foreach($parts as $part)
        <td>{{ $part }}</td>
    @endforeach
</tr>
```

This ensures that even if the data changes from three items to five, your table adjusts automatically without modifying the controller or view logic.

### When to Move Logic to the Controller

Currently, you are performing string manipulation in the Blade template. While acceptable for simple views, it is generally better practice to process data in the controller or a model method. Moving this logic to `HomeController` keeps the view clean and ensures consistent parsing across different parts of your application.

```php
public function index()
{
    $test = Test::all();

    foreach ($test as $row) {
        $cleanData = str_replace(['{', '}'], '', $row->data);
        $row->parsedValues = array_filter(array_map('trim', explode(',', $cleanData)));
    }

    return view('home')->with('test', $test);
}
```

By accessing `$row->parsedValues` in your Blade file, you separate concerns effectively. This pattern is essential for maintaining code quality as the application scales.

For further guidance on handling complex data structures and optimizing Laravel views, you might refer to resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com).
